export const blocks: PaperBlock[] = [
  {"type": "p", "text": "Abstract SunRey is an AI-native financial and economic operating system whose security problem spans more than confidentiality. It must protect money movement, AI-initiated actions, blockchain state, custody, personal information, market infrastructure, provider connections, machine identities, governance and the software supply chain without allowing any one security mechanism to become a universal authority. This paper describes a layered architecture in which post-quantum readiness, cryptographic agility, zero-trust access, deterministic authorization, canonical ledgers, sovereign blockchain state, key-purpose separation, protected data domains, evidence-producing execution and operational resilience operate as one security fabric. The architecture is deliberately more conservative than the phrase \"quantum-proof.\" SunRey source material describes an algorithm registry, security KeyProvider abstractions, hybrid post-quantum migration concepts, ML-KEM/ML-DSA/SLH-DSA testnet-oriented ports, signer safety, HSM/KMS interfaces, zero-trust controls and external assurance gates. Those elements support a credible post-quantum migration program, but they do not establish that every production communication, wallet, validator or custody path is already post-quantum protected. [S4]"},
  {"type": "callout", "label": "Claim Discipline", "text": "This paper distinguishes architecture defined, code implemented, sandbox/testnet qualification, production configuration, external assurance and production activation. No layer is described as certified or \"quantum-proof\" without evidence. Publication boundary This document is a public architecture paper. It intentionally excludes credentials, private network addressing, sensitive administrative endpoints, firewall specifics, recovery secrets, key ceremony secrets, provider credentials and confidential vulnerability findings. It is not a penetration-test report, certification, legal opinion or production-security attestation. External standards check The standards references in this edition were checked against current NIST, CISA, NSA and IETF materials available in September 2026. NIST FIPS 203, 204 and 205 standardize ML-KEM, ML-DSA and SLH-DSA; NIST SP 800-227 provides KEM guidance; NIST CSWP 39upd1 addresses crypto agility; and IETF RFC 10024, published in August 2026, defines hybrid TLS 1.3 key agreements combining ML-KEM with ECDHE. [E1-E8] Report Map Section Topic Executive Summary Security Thesis and Claim Discipline Threat Model for a Post-AI Economic System Defense-in-Depth Security Architecture Quantum Risk and Long-Lived Financial Data Crypto Agility as an Architectural Requirement Post-Quantum Standards Landscape - 2026 Data in Transit and Hybrid TLS Data at Rest, Secrets and Protected Storage Key Management and Roots of Trust SunRey Blockchain Security Validator, P2P and Consensus Security Transaction Security and Proof of Permission AI Financial Agent Security Internal Zero-Trust Architecture External Provider and API Security Human Information Network and Data Security Wallet and Digital-Asset Custody Security SunRey Exchange Security Oracle and External-Data Security Interoperability and Bridge Security Cloud and Infrastructure Security Software Supply-Chain Security Formal, Fuzz and Adversarial Assurance Observability and Security Operations Incident Response, Recovery and Resilience Security Governance and Separation of Duties Post-Quantum Migration Roadmap Post-Quantum Maturity Model Section Topic External Assurance and Production Gates Strategic Differentiation Conclusion A Glossary B References"},
  {"type": "callout", "label": "Reading Guide", "text": "Sections 1-4 establish the trust model. Sections 5-10 define cryptographic and post-quantum architecture."},
  {"type": "p", "text": "Sections 11-23 apply security to the blockchain, AI, data, providers, custody, Exchange and infrastructure. Sections 24-30 address assurance, operations and migration."},
  {"type": "section", "n": "01", "label": "", "title": "Executive Summary"},
  {"type": "p", "text": "The objective is not merely stronger encryption; it is controlled economic authority under changing cryptographic assumptions. SunRey combines financial accounts, global money movement, AI financial agents, a proprietary blockchain, SunRey Coin, MoonRey Coin, an economic Exchange, protected human-information rights, wallets, custody, oracles and productive-capacity markets. That combination creates a security perimeter that is broader than the perimeter of either a conventional bank application or a standalone blockchain. A compromise can originate at the user device, an AI model, an API, a provider, a validator, a software dependency, a key-management system, a database, an oracle or a governance process. The architecture therefore cannot rely on one perimeter, one signature system or one notion of trust. The central design rule is separation of authority. SunRey source architecture requires consequential actions to flow through an authorization spine: ActionIntent → Compliance Kernel → signed Execution Authority → Financial Fabric -> canonical Ledger or blockchain settlement → Evidence Vault. AI agents may discover and propose; policy determines permission; deterministic risk controls determine exposure; controlled infrastructure executes; canonical state records the result. [S1][S2][S4]"},
  {"type": "callout", "label": "Security Invariant", "text": "Compromise of one layer should not automatically compromise economic authority in another. A stolen API credential should not become a signing key. A model compromise should not become a wallet compromise. A valid signature should not automatically mean a transaction is policy-authorized. Post-quantum readiness is integrated into this model as a transition discipline. The SunRey architecture reports algorithm registries, provider ports, distinct key purposes, hybrid migration concepts and testnet-oriented ML-KEM/MLDSA/SLH-DSA integration points. The right public claim is therefore crypto-agile and post-quantum-ready by architecture, with production use gated by qualification, provider support and independent review. [S4]"},
  {"type": "figure", "caption": "Figure 1. SunRey defense-in-depth architecture. Cryptography is one layer inside a larger authority and evidence model.", "src": "/papers/figures/10-sunrey-post-quantum-security-fig-01.webp", "w": 1600, "h": 996},
  {"type": "section", "n": "02", "label": "", "title": "Security Thesis and Claim Discipline"},
  {"type": "p", "text": "A security white paper should distinguish what exists in architecture from what is operationally proven. 2.1 Four security questions Can an actor or service prove who or what it is? Is that actor authorized for this specific action, purpose and context? Can the system preserve confidentiality and authenticity under current and future cryptographic assumptions? Can the platform reconstruct what happened and recover without creating a second source of financial truth? Traditional cybersecurity often stops after identity and cryptography. SunRey adds a separate economic-authority layer because authenticated systems can still be wrong, compromised, misconfigured or legally unauthorized. This matters particularly for autonomous agents and external providers: a correct TLS session proves a secure channel to an endpoint; it does not prove that the provider response should create money, move funds, change a user's risk mandate or alter protocol governance. 2.2 Maturity states State Meaning Permitted public wording Architecture defined Controls, interfaces and authority boundaries are specified. \"Designed for\" / \"architecture supports\" Implemented in code A control exists in the repository. \"Implemented component\" when verified Sandbox / testnet qualified The control has passed bounded nonproduction tests. \"Qualified in controlled environment\" Production configured Real keys, providers and infrastructure are bound. \"Production-configured\" only with evidence Externally assured Independent review/audit evidence exists. Name the scope and reviewer; avoid blanket claims Production active The exact control is operating in the live service. Scope claim to the specific path and version"},
  {"type": "callout", "label": "Public Claim Rule", "text": "Never compress these states into a single statement such as \"SunRey is quantum-proof\" or \"SunRey is fully postquantum encrypted.\""},
  {"type": "section", "n": "03", "label": "", "title": "Threat Model for a Post-AI Economic System"},
  {"type": "p", "text": "The attack surface includes both cyber compromise and authority confusion. SunRey must defend not only against classic confidentiality, integrity and availability failures, but also against an attacker causing one subsystem to be accepted as an authority it was never meant to hold. The highest-risk failures therefore include both direct compromise and cross-plane privilege escalation. Threat family Example attack Primary authority at risk Cryptographic Private-key theft, downgrade, weak or deprecated algorithm Confidentiality, signatures, service identity Identity Credential theft, device takeover, impersonation User or operator identity AI Prompt injection, malicious tool call, model compromise Proposal and decision-support plane Financial Unauthorized payment, duplicate settlement, ledger manipulation Canonical financial state Blockchain Validator key theft, double-signing, consensus fault Protocol finality and native assets Wallet / custody Unauthorized signing or recovery abuse Digital-asset control Exchange Order abuse, market manipulation, settlement inconsistency Market integrity and custody linkage Data Exfiltration or purpose expansion Human information rights and privacy Oracle Spoofed, stale or duplicated economic facts External-fact quality Provider Compromised bank/KYC/market-data/API dependency External service trust Supply chain Compromised dependency, build or release artifact Software integrity Governance Captured approval, malicious upgrade or key misuse Protocol/control-plane authority"},
  {"type": "figure", "caption": "Figure 2. A threat-control-evidence chain suitable for financial infrastructure.", "src": "/papers/figures/10-sunrey-post-quantum-security-fig-02.webp", "w": 1600, "h": 729},
  {"type": "section", "n": "04", "label": "", "title": "Defense-in-Depth Security Architecture"},
  {"type": "p", "text": "Security controls are layered around identity, policy, execution and evidence rather than around a single perimeter. The SunRey security model is consistent with zero-trust architecture: internal network location is not itself a reason to trust a user, workload or service. NIST SP 800-207 similarly defines zero trust around removing implicit trust based on network location and protecting resources through explicit authentication and authorization. [E9] 4.1 Security planes User and device plane - identity verification, MFA/passkeys, trusted-device state, session controls and step-up authentication. Edge and API plane - TLS, API authentication, request validation, idempotency, rate limiting, replay controls and browserorigin policy. Application and AI plane - workload identity, least privilege, capability tokens, model/tool restrictions and bounded proposal generation. Policy and risk plane - Compliance Kernel, jurisdiction packs, purpose controls, deterministic risk limits and kill switches. Execution plane - signed, short-lived Execution Authority as the gate to consequential state change. State plane - canonical financial Ledger, SunRey Blockchain finality, custody and immutable/reconstructable evidence. Operations plane - observability, anomaly detection, incident response, backup/restore, security review and release governance."},
  {"type": "callout", "label": "Defense-In-Depth Principle", "text": "Security mechanisms should fail independently. If one control weakens, downstream controls should still constrain"},
  {"type": "p", "text": "the blast radius and preserve evidence."},
  {"type": "section", "n": "05", "label": "", "title": "Quantum Risk and Long-Lived Financial Data"},
  {"type": "p", "text": "The most immediate PQC problem is migration planning, not speculation about the date of a cryptographically relevant quantum computer. Current public-key systems used for key establishment and digital signatures are the primary migration concern for a future cryptanalytically relevant quantum computer. CISA, NSA and NIST have urged organizations to plan early because migration is large, dependencies are difficult to inventory, and data captured today may retain value long enough to be attacked later - the \"harvest now, decrypt later\" problem. [E6] Financial systems contain unusually long-lived assets: identity records, legal agreements, transaction evidence, custody records, private economic data, audit histories and institutional credentials. Even if a future cryptographic break is years away, a system that cannot locate its quantum-vulnerable dependencies cannot make a controlled transition when required. 5.1 SunRey cryptographic exposure classes Exposure class Examples Migration priority Long-lived confidentiality Vault exports, archived financial evidence, backups, sensitive institutional records Prioritize discovery and protection strategy Long-lived authenticity Governance attestations, release provenance, archival signatures Define re-signing / renewal / evidence policy Transactional authentication TLS, service identity, provider API authentication Crypto-agile protocol and vendor readiness Blockchain signatures Wallet, validator, governance and attestation signatures Versioned protocol transition; avoid uncoordinated changes Third-party dependency Banks, custody, cloud, HSM/KMS, mobile platforms Vendor roadmap and acceptance evidence"},
  {"type": "callout", "label": "Why Prepare Now", "text": "The security objective is to reduce migration surprise. Inventory, abstraction, versioning and vendor coordination"},
  {"type": "p", "text": "can be implemented before production deployment of any specific PQ algorithm."},
  {"type": "section", "n": "06", "label": "", "title": "Crypto Agility as an Architectural Requirement"},
  {"type": "p", "text": "Post-quantum security is sustainable only if algorithms can change without redesigning the economic system. NIST defines crypto agility as the capabilities needed to replace and adapt cryptographic algorithms across protocols, applications, software, hardware, firmware and infrastructure while preserving security and ongoing operations. Its updated 2026 guidance emphasizes operational mechanisms and trade-offs rather than a single universal implementation pattern. [E5] SunRey source architecture aligns with this direction by describing an algorithm registry, provider ports, established primitives, hybrid post-quantum migration and a ban on homegrown cryptography. [S4] The architectural goal is that financial, blockchain and data semantics remain stable while cryptographic suites can be versioned, qualified and replaced. 6.1 Algorithm Registry Registry field Purpose Algorithm / suite identifier Canonical reference used by protocol and evidence Algorithm family KEM, digital signature, symmetric encryption, hash, MAC, key derivation Lifecycle state Research, qualified, approved, deprecated, disabled Environment scope Development, testnet, sandbox, preproduction, production Key purpose Service identity, wallet, validator, governance, encryption, release signing Provider / module Software library, HSM, KMS, custody signer or platform implementation Version / parameters Prevents ambiguous or silent changes Compatibility profile Protocol and external-party requirements Evidence links Test vectors, assurance results, approval records"},
  {"type": "callout", "label": "No Homegrown Cryptography", "text": "SunRey may innovate in economic state, authorization and protocol composition; cryptographic primitives should"},
  {"type": "p", "text": "come from established, reviewed standards and implementations."},
  {"type": "section", "n": "07", "label": "", "title": "Post-Quantum Standards Landscape - 2026"},
  {"type": "p", "text": "Standards are mature enough to plan against, but deployment choices still require system-level engineering and assurance. NIST approved the first three primary PQC FIPS standards in August 2024. FIPS 203 specifies ML-KEM for key establishment; FIPS 204 specifies ML-DSA for digital signatures; and FIPS 205 specifies SLH-DSA, a stateless hashbased signature scheme. [E1-E3] NIST SP 800-227, finalized in September 2025, provides recommendations for KEM use. [E4] Standard Role SunRey relevance"},
  {"type": "callout", "label": "Fips 203 - Ml-Kem", "text": "Post-quantum key encapsulation Candidate foundation for qualified key-establishment paths; not proof of current production use"},
  {"type": "callout", "label": "Fips 204 - Ml-Dsa", "text": "Post-quantum digital signatures Candidate for versioned signing roles subject to performance, HSM and protocol qualification"},
  {"type": "callout", "label": "Fips 205 - Slh-Dsa", "text": "Stateless hash-based signatures Alternative signature family with different performance/size characteristics"},
  {"type": "callout", "label": "Sp 800-227", "text": "Secure KEM implementation guidance Useful for implementation and combiner design review CSWP 39upd1 Crypto-agility strategies and practices Supports algorithm abstraction, inventory and transition discipline"},
  {"type": "callout", "label": "Rfc 10024", "text": "Hybrid PQ/traditional TLS 1.3 key agreements Relevant to external/internal transport migration and compatibility testing IETF RFC 10024 became a Standards Track RFC in August 2026. It defines TLS 1.3 hybrid groups that combine MLKEM with ECDHE, including X25519MLKEM768 and NIST-curve variants. This is directly relevant to SunRey's hybridtransport strategy because it provides a standards-based path for combining conventional and post-quantum key agreement during migration. [E8] STANDARD AVAILABLE != PRODUCTION DEPLOYED The existence of a standard does not prove that a particular SunRey service, HSM, wallet, validator or provider has implemented and qualified it."},
  {"type": "section", "n": "08", "label": "", "title": "Data in Transit and Hybrid TLS"},
  {"type": "p", "text": "Transport protection must evolve without breaking provider interoperability or weakening identity and authorization. SunRey depends on many communications paths: mobile/web clients to API edge; service-to-service calls; provider adapters; custody signers; market-data feeds; oracle connections; validator/P2P links; operator access and external interoperability. Each path should be inventoried by protocol, endpoint class, certificate/signature dependency, confidentiality lifetime and external compatibility requirement."},
  {"type": "figure", "caption": "Figure 3. Post-quantum migration should proceed as a governed lifecycle across cryptographic dependencies.", "src": "/papers/figures/10-sunrey-post-quantum-security-fig-03.webp", "w": 1600, "h": 933},
  {"type": "p", "text": "8.1 Hybrid migration Hybrid key agreement is useful where organizations want protection that does not depend solely on a new PQ primitive while maintaining transition compatibility. RFC 10024 standardizes specific PQ/traditional hybrid TLS 1.3 groups. SunRey can evaluate these or successor standards within a qualified transport profile, measuring handshake size, latency, CPU cost, certificate/tooling compatibility, provider support and failure behavior. [E8] 8.2 Transport rules No cleartext internal assumption merely because traffic is \"inside\" a cloud or VPC. Mutual or workload-authenticated channels where service identity is consequential. Provider endpoints treated as external evidence sources even when TLS authentication succeeds. Protocol downgrade and legacy-suite use observable and policy-controlled. Transport configuration versioned and associated with deployment evidence."},
  {"type": "section", "n": "09", "label": "", "title": "Data at Rest, Secrets and Protected Storage"},
  {"type": "p", "text": "Encryption at rest is necessary but insufficient; key separation, access policy and purpose limitation determine the real trust boundary. The SunRey architecture places particularly sensitive human source data outside the public blockchain and inside protected storage domains. The original architecture also calls for independently encrypted Vault categories, separate encryption keys where appropriate, a Purpose Firewall and a revocable Consent Ledger. [S2] This reduces the risk that economic coordination requires permanent publication of raw personal data. 9.1 Protected storage classes Data class Security objective Preferred control pattern Financial ledger / account state Integrity, recoverability, access control Encrypted databases, role-separated access, immutable journal semantics, backup/restore Human information source data Confidentiality, purpose limitation, revocation Segmented Vault, independent keys, policy enforcement, clean-room compute Audit / evidence Integrity and reconstruction Tamper-evident or WORM-oriented storage, signed/hash-linked records Provider credentials Confidentiality and rotation Secret references, managed secret stores, least privilege, no source-code secrets Backups Confidentiality and availability Encrypted, access-separated, tested restore, offsystem copies where appropriate"},
  {"type": "callout", "label": "Data Rule", "text": "Encryption answers \"who can read the bytes?\" Purpose controls answer \"who may use the information, for what purpose, and for how long?\" SunRey needs both."},
  {"type": "section", "n": "10", "label": "", "title": "Key Management and Roots of Trust"},
  {"type": "p", "text": "Key material should be purpose-separated, lifecycle-managed and incapable of silently becoming universal authority. SunRey source architecture describes a Security KeyProvider/root security abstraction and later HSM/KMS signer ports, approval controls, recovery/rotation/delegated keys and production key-management evidence as a launch gate. [S4][S6] This is the correct decomposition: applications should depend on controlled signing/encryption interfaces rather than directly handling unrestricted key material."},
  {"type": "figure", "caption": "Figure 4. Key-purpose separation prevents a single credential from becoming a universal security root.", "src": "/papers/figures/10-sunrey-post-quantum-security-fig-04.webp", "w": 1600, "h": 933},
  {"type": "p", "text": "10.1 Key lifecycle 1. 2. 3. 4. 5. Generate or import using an approved provider and documented entropy/source requirements. Bind the key to a purpose, environment, owner/controller, algorithm suite and policy state. Restrict use through service identity, human approval, rate/amount constraints or threshold control as appropriate. Rotate and revoke without rewriting the economic object model. Preserve auditable evidence of creation, use, rotation, compromise response and retirement. KEY POSSESSION != ECONOMIC AUTHORITY A cryptographically valid signature proves that a key was used. A separate policy layer should still determine whether that key was permitted to authorize the requested economic state change."},
  {"type": "section", "n": "11", "label": "", "title": "SunRey Blockchain Security"},
  {"type": "p", "text": "A sovereign economic state machine has to protect actors, rights, policy and evidence - not only token balances. SunRey Blockchain is designed as the sovereign base layer for native SunRey economic objects, SunRey Coin, MoonRey Coin and core protocol state. The architecture treats human, enterprise, AI-agent, robot, device and productive-asset identities; rights and consent; provenance; policy/jurisdiction; productive capacity; settlement and evidence as first-class protocol concepts. [S5]"},
  {"type": "figure", "caption": "Figure 5. Security domains within the SunRey sovereign economic state machine.", "src": "/papers/figures/10-sunrey-post-quantum-security-fig-05.webp", "w": 1600, "h": 960},
  {"type": "p", "text": "11.1 Deterministic execution and canonical state A security-sensitive execution environment should favor deterministic state transitions, canonical encoding, explicit resource accounting, versioned transaction envelopes and reproducible validation. Source build reports describe canonical binary encoding, no floating-point consensus values, deterministic execution and BFT-oriented finality work. [S4] These controls reduce cross-implementation ambiguity and make replay, hashing and signing behavior tractable. 11.2 Rich transaction security Because SunRey transactions can carry identity, rights, purpose, jurisdiction, consent references, oracle state and evidence requirements, protocol validation can reject transactions that are cryptographically valid but contextually unauthorized. This is an important distinction from a narrow account-balance model. [S5]"},
  {"type": "section", "n": "12", "label": "", "title": "Validator, P2P and Consensus Security"},
  {"type": "p", "text": "Validator safety depends on keys, operator identity, network topology, accountability and controlled upgrades. The source architecture describes validator operators as bonded named principals, separate consensus and P2P keys, epoch-based set changes, equivocation evidence, authenticated node identities, sentry-capable topology, consensuspriority traffic and RPC separation. It also reports signer fencing, double-sign protection and BFT-style rounds in the testnet/protocol program. [S4] 12.1 Validator threat controls Threat Control objective Evidence Validator key theft HSM/KMS or controlled signer, key-purpose separation, rotation/revocation Signer logs, key state, incident record Double signing / equivocation Signer fencing, height/round safety, evidence and penalties/governance response Consensus evidence P2P DoS Sentry topology, peer policy, RPC separation, resource controls Network telemetry and alerts Malicious upgrade Versioned governance, activation coordinates, threshold human authority Signed governance package and release provenance Consensus divergence Deterministic encoding/execution and bounded protocol versions Cross-node test vectors and replay evidence"},
  {"type": "callout", "label": "Governance Boundary", "text": "A software release signature or AI recommendation should not itself authorize a protocol upgrade. Upgrade authority belongs to explicit governance."},
  {"type": "section", "n": "13", "label": "", "title": "Transaction Security and Proof of Permission"},
  {"type": "p", "text": "Every consequential state change should carry both cryptographic validity and contextual authorization. SunRey's Proof of Permission model requires six authorization classes - Identity, Authority, Jurisdiction, Compliance, Risk and Purpose - before the Compliance Kernel may issue an Execution Authority. The authorization posture is monotonic: a request may become more restrictive as evidence changes, but should not silently relax mid-flight. [S1]"},
  {"type": "figure", "caption": "Figure 6. AI and user requests become typed ActionIntents; deterministic controls retain execution authority.", "src": "/papers/figures/10-sunrey-post-quantum-security-fig-06.webp", "w": 1600, "h": 827},
  {"type": "p", "text": "13.1 Proof-Carrying Financial Events The Evidence Vault model records the ActionIntent together with identity state, KYC/AML/sanctions/fraud results, legal entity, jurisdiction, product/account eligibility, agreement/disclosure versions, agent capability, model version, risk result, policy version, approval decision, execution result, ledger entries, settlement state, timestamps and cryptographic references. The target question is mechanically simple: \"Why was this action permitted at that exact time?\" [S2]"},
  {"type": "callout", "label": "Transaction Security", "text": "A secure transaction is not merely signed. It is signed by the right authority, for the right purpose, in the right"},
  {"type": "p", "text": "jurisdiction, under the right risk and compliance state, and recorded in a canonical ledger with evidence."},
  {"type": "section", "n": "14", "label": "", "title": "AI Financial Agent Security"},
  {"type": "p", "text": "The security objective is to gain autonomous reasoning without granting autonomous financial sovereignty. SunRey's Financial Agent architecture deliberately separates analysis from execution. Agents reason over customerauthorized context and emit ActionIntents. Capability tokens define allowed and forbidden actions, account classes, limits, jurisdiction, expiry and revocation. The deterministic Risk Engine outranks all trading AI, and models are expected to progress through research, testing, independent validation, shadow/paper/canary stages before production use. [S2] 14.1 Principal AI threats Threat Example Control Prompt injection External content attempts to cause a privileged tool call Treat model output as untrusted proposal; enforce tool capability in infrastructure Data exfiltration Agent attempts to expose private Vault context to an external model Purpose firewall, data minimization, model/provider boundary Hallucinated action Model invents beneficiary, asset or permission state Canonical data lookup plus deterministic validation Privilege escalation Agent requests action outside mandate Capability token and Kernel denial Compromised external model Provider behavior becomes malicious or unavailable Provider isolation, structured outputs, fallback/degraded mode, no execution credentials Poisoned market data Adversarial data biases recommendation External data trust, corroboration, provenance, confidence state"},
  {"type": "callout", "label": "Ai Security Principle", "text": "A model compromise should degrade the quality of proposals, not silently grant custody, ledger, compliance, governance or wallet authority."},
  {"type": "section", "n": "15", "label": "", "title": "Internal Zero-Trust Architecture"},
  {"type": "p", "text": "Internal does not mean trusted. SunRey's internal security baseline calls for zero trust, least privilege, strong service identity, mTLS, HSM/KMS-backed keys, MFA/passkeys, device binding, privileged access management, immutable audit logging, network segmentation, secret rotation and no secrets in source code. [S2][S6] This closely matches the resource-centric and identity-centric principles in NIST's zero-trust guidance. [E9]"},
  {"type": "figure", "caption": "Figure 7. External services, SunRey-controlled services and protected data remain separate trust domains.", "src": "/papers/figures/10-sunrey-post-quantum-security-fig-07.webp", "w": 1600, "h": 916},
  {"type": "p", "text": "15.1 Service-to-service trust A service should not receive privilege because its IP address is \"internal.\" Workload identity, explicit authorization, policy context and narrow service roles should be evaluated at each sensitive boundary. This matters in a microservice environment because lateral movement after a single workload compromise is often more damaging than the initial foothold."},
  {"type": "section", "n": "16", "label": "", "title": "External Provider and API Security"},
  {"type": "p", "text": "External integrations should be replaceable, authenticated and unable to promote themselves into internal financial authority. Banks, payment rails, card processors, KYC/AML vendors, custody providers, market-data feeds, oracles and AI providers sit behind SunRey-owned interfaces in the productization architecture. Provider runtime controls include lifecycle state, secret references, environment isolation, capability routing, health, timeouts, retries, circuit breakers, failover, kill switches and certification. [S6] 16.1 External trust rules Authenticate the provider and transport, but treat the provider response as evidence rather than financial authority. Normalize vendor-specific status into canonical SunRey states before product logic consumes it. Use scoped credentials and secret references; avoid embedding provider secrets in application source or frontend code. Verify inbound webhooks with signatures/timestamps/nonces where supported and make consumers idempotent. Fail closed for capabilities whose security, legal or provider evidence is missing. Maintain provider dependency, concentration-risk and exit-plan metadata for critical services."},
  {"type": "callout", "label": "External Evidence Rule", "text": "Provider success may inform a decision. It should not mint native assets, bypass the Compliance Kernel, rewrite"},
  {"type": "p", "text": "the Ledger or silently activate a regulated capability."},
  {"type": "section", "n": "17", "label": "", "title": "Human Information Network and Data Security"},
  {"type": "p", "text": "SunRey protects rights around information without turning the blockchain into a permanent public repository of sensitive source data. SunRey's data architecture keeps sensitive source data off-chain and uses protected storage for user-linked information. The blockchain may carry suitable rights, consent, provenance, attestations, policy references and settlement evidence rather than raw personal records. [S4][S5] 17.1 Purpose-bound access The Personal Data Vault, Consent Ledger and Purpose Firewall are security controls as much as privacy features. Every sensitive-data access should state a purpose, scope, requester, data categories, duration, jurisdiction, model class where relevant, compensation terms and revocation state. Possession of information never automatically implies permission to use it. [S1][S2] 17.2 Compute-to-data For approved analytics and research, the architecture prefers computation-to-data over indiscriminate data export. A controlled clean-room environment can accept a query or model and return only permitted outputs. This reduces copying, limits data sprawl and provides a stronger place to enforce privacy thresholds, logging and purpose policy."},
  {"type": "callout", "label": "Data Minimization", "text": "The strongest encryption strategy is still weaker than never moving unnecessary sensitive data into a broader trust"},
  {"type": "p", "text": "domain in the first place."},
  {"type": "section", "n": "18", "label": "", "title": "Wallet and Digital-Asset Custody Security"},
  {"type": "p", "text": "Custody is a distinct authority domain with its own keys, approvals, limits and recovery processes. The architecture calls for MPC and HSM-backed signing options, cold/warm/hot wallet patterns, withdrawal controls, rate limits, allowlists, transaction simulation, anomaly detection and strict separation of customer digital assets from corporate assets. [S2] The build architecture further describes canonical custody, signer providers, approval controls, finality handling and submission-ambiguity management. [S4] 18.1 Custody control objectives Private-key material should be non-exportable or tightly controlled where institutional infrastructure permits. Signing policy should include purpose, amount, destination, velocity, account class and approval state. Recovery should require stronger authority than ordinary transaction signing. Submission ambiguity should not cause duplicate transfer attempts; idempotency and finality tracking are required. Exchange balances should not become a competing custody or ledger truth source."},
  {"type": "callout", "label": "Custody Invariant", "text": "The Exchange may reserve or request assets; canonical custody and chain/ledger state determine whether those assets actually exist and move."},
  {"type": "section", "n": "19", "label": "", "title": "SunRey Exchange Security"},
  {"type": "p", "text": "A universal economic exchange introduces market-integrity, custody, data-rights and settlement risks beyond ordinary token trading. SunRey Exchange is designed to host multiple market families, including digital assets, human-information rights, intelligence/compute and productive capacity. Orders may carry identity, rights, jurisdiction, purpose, consent, provenance, delivery conditions and policy state in addition to price and quantity. [S5] 19.1 Exchange security layers Layer Security requirement Order entry Authenticated actor, replay/idempotency controls, policy eligibility, market limits Matching Deterministic price-time or market-specific matching; no hidden balance authority Custody Pre-trade reservation and canonical custody linkage Settlement Atomic or carefully orchestrated DVP; explicit failure/recovery state Surveillance Market-abuse detectors, restrictions, concentration/velocity checks, kill switches Data-rights markets Purpose, consent, privacy threshold and delivery evidence Operational controls Pause by market, asset, customer, provider or jurisdiction without disabling the entire platform The cryptographic problem is therefore only one component. The Exchange must also defend against logically valid but unauthorized market actions, stale or manipulated external state, inconsistent settlement, compromised custody, duplicated execution and policy drift."},
  {"type": "section", "n": "20", "label": "", "title": "Oracle and External-Data Security"},
  {"type": "p", "text": "External information may inform economic state without becoming monetary authority. SunRey relies on external observations for markets, productive capacity, asset state, delivery and economic facts. The security problem is not merely authenticating the API; it is establishing whether an observation is authoritative enough, fresh enough and independent enough to support a specific action. 20.1 External data trust Source identity and authorization Freshness and timestamp policy Provenance and transformation lineage Cross-source corroboration and source independence Outlier/anomaly handling Provider health and degraded-mode state Canonical event identity and anti-double-counting Explicit separation between evidence, valuation and monetary authority"},
  {"type": "callout", "label": "Oracle Rule", "text": "A signed oracle message can prove what an oracle said. It does not prove that the statement is true, sufficiently"},
  {"type": "p", "text": "corroborated, non-duplicative or authorized to alter native monetary supply."},
  {"type": "section", "n": "21", "label": "", "title": "Interoperability and Bridge Security"},
  {"type": "p", "text": "External chains may interoperate without becoming the native source of truth for SunRey economic state. The SunRey architecture keeps external chains outside the authoritative base layer and treats relayers as untrusted. Source documents explicitly prefer proof-oriented interoperability over trusted-multisig bridge roots and reject wrappedfiat shortcuts as an authority substitute. [S4][S5] 21.1 Bridge control framework Risk Control direction Relayer compromise Verify proofs/finality rather than trust relayer assertions Replay Bind messages to source chain, destination, nonce/sequence, asset and domain External reorganization Model finality assumptions and delay/limit settlement appropriately Wrapped-asset insolvency Separate representation risk from native-asset truth; require reserve/custody evidence where applicable Bridge key compromise Threshold governance, rate limits, emergency suspension, narrow key scope Protocol bug Allowlist supported pathways, bounded limits, staged rollout and kill switches"},
  {"type": "callout", "label": "Interop Principle", "text": "Interoperability should import verifiable state or authorized economic relationships - not import another network's"},
  {"type": "p", "text": "security assumptions as SunRey's root of trust."},
  {"type": "section", "n": "22", "label": "", "title": "Cloud and Infrastructure Security"},
  {"type": "p", "text": "Production infrastructure should preserve one canonical state path, strict network exposure and environment isolation. SunRey's deployment architecture has used multiple infrastructure environments during productization, but the security rule remains one authoritative state for each domain. Persistent databases, blockchain nodes, custody services and economic state should not be duplicated in ways that create competing financial truth. [S6] 22.1 Infrastructure control classes Cloud/hosting accounts protected by strong operator identity, MFA and least privilege. Databases and internal services not directly exposed to the public Internet unless specifically required. Reverse proxy / edge services enforce HTTPS, request limits and intended origin boundaries. Infrastructure-as-code and versioned configuration reduce undocumented drift. Secrets stored outside source code and rotated through managed systems. Environment separation prevents sandbox/testnet credentials or fixtures from silently becoming production authority. Backups encrypted, access-separated and regularly restore-tested."},
  {"type": "callout", "label": "Publication Safety", "text": "This paper intentionally omits IP addresses, secret names/values, private ports, administrative endpoints and detailed firewall configuration."},
  {"type": "section", "n": "23", "label": "", "title": "Software Supply-Chain Security"},
  {"type": "p", "text": "The release pipeline is itself part of the financial-security perimeter. SunRey build reports describe architecture linting, TypeScript and Rust qualification, secret scans, formal/fuzz/adversarial testing, OCI artifact construction, SBOM/provenance and release-candidate gates. [S4][S6] These practices are consistent with the principle behind NIST's Secure Software Development Framework: secure development practices should be integrated into the SDLC rather than added only after release. [E10] 23.1 Source-to-deployment chain Stage Security requirement Source Protected main branch, review, code ownership, no secrets Dependencies Lockfiles, provenance awareness, vulnerability monitoring Build Clean/reproducible process where practical, architecture and type/lint gates Test Unit/integration/E2E, financial invariants, security, fuzz/adversarial checks Artifact Immutable digest, SBOM, provenance/attestation, exact release identity Promotion Environment-specific approvals and external production gates Deployment Pinned release; no floating \"latest\" as authority Rollback Known-good artifact and state-compatible recovery procedure CODE EXISTS != CAPABILITY AUTHORIZED A production-capable code path can remain disabled until legal, provider, security, governance and operational evidence exists."},
  {"type": "section", "n": "24", "label": "", "title": "Formal, Fuzz and Adversarial Assurance"},
  {"type": "p", "text": "Testing should target both implementation defects and failures of authority boundaries. Source build reports describe formal models, fuzzing, adversarial-range testing, signer safety tests and extensive regression suites. [S4] The publication claim should remain bounded: internal tests are valuable engineering evidence, but they are not substitutes for independent review of cryptography, consensus, custody or production infrastructure. 24.1 Assurance portfolio Unit tests for deterministic functions and cryptographic adapters. Property tests for invariants such as balanced ledgers, idempotency and monotonic authorization. Fuzzing of parsers, transaction envelopes, network messages and state-transition surfaces. Adversarial tests for double-signing, replay, privilege escalation, invalid state transitions, provider failures and malformed evidence. Concurrency/crash testing for settlement, jobs, wallet operations and persistence. Chaos tests for provider loss, cell isolation, database/network disruption and degraded modes. Independent penetration and protocol reviews before live activation."},
  {"type": "callout", "label": "Assurance Principle", "text": "The relevant question is not \"How many tests exist?\" but \"Which failure modes and authority invariants have been"},
  {"type": "p", "text": "exercised, under what assumptions, and with what independent evidence?\""},
  {"type": "section", "n": "25", "label": "", "title": "Observability and Security Operations"},
  {"type": "p", "text": "A secure economic system must be able to detect divergence and reconstruct cause without giving the monitoring plane execution authority. SunRey's operations architecture calls for metrics, logs, traces, SLIs/SLOs, alerts, incident records, control-room views, runbooks and provider/financial/security operations APIs. [S6] These signals should be correlated around stable transaction, user, agent, model, provider, release and evidence identifiers so that operational events can be traced across domains. 25.1 High-value detections Repeated authentication and step-up failures Unexpected privilege or role changes Abnormal transaction velocity or destination changes Ledger reconciliation breaks Validator or signer anomalies Custody approval/signing failures Market surveillance events Provider timeout/error pattern shifts AI tool-call denials or unusual proposal patterns Backup/restore failures Secret-access anomalies Unexpected production-gate or configuration changes"},
  {"type": "callout", "label": "Observability Boundary", "text": "Monitoring may reveal, classify and recommend. It should not silently become a money-moving, governance or protocol-upgrade authority."},
  {"type": "section", "n": "26", "label": "", "title": "Incident Response, Recovery and Resilience"},
  {"type": "p", "text": "Recovery must restore trusted authority, not merely restore service availability. SunRey security guidance includes multi-region capability, database replication, point-in-time recovery, immutable backups, backup verification, recovery exercises, provider-dependency mapping and independent kill switches across trading, payments, external transfers, crypto withdrawals, Exchange, data markets, agents, customers and jurisdictions. [S2] 26.1 Incident recovery sequence 1. Contain the affected trust domain and activate the narrowest appropriate kill switch. 2. Preserve evidence and establish the last known trustworthy state. 3. Revoke or rotate compromised credentials/keys and quarantine affected providers or services. 4. Restore from verified state or replay canonical ledgers/events as designed. 5. Reconcile financial and blockchain state before reopening execution. 6. Requalify the repaired environment and document accepted residual risk. 7. Only then reactivate capability through the appropriate human/governance process."},
  {"type": "callout", "label": "Resilience Principle", "text": "Availability without trustworthy state is not recovery. Financial and blockchain systems must restore both service"},
  {"type": "p", "text": "and confidence in canonical economic state."},
  {"type": "section", "n": "27", "label": "", "title": "Security Governance and Separation of Duties"},
  {"type": "p", "text": "No single AI, administrator, signer or deployment system should become protocol governance. SunRey architecture separates protocol governance, security operations, infrastructure, compliance, custody, financial operations, software release, validator operations and AI/model governance. Source documentation calls for explicit protocol versions and activation coordinates with human threshold authority. [S4] 27.1 Authority domains Authority May authorize Should not independently authorize Security operations Containment, credential revocation, security response Monetary policy or arbitrary fund movement Compliance / policy Eligibility, jurisdiction and regulatory conditions Custody signing or protocol upgrades Custody Approved transaction signing within policy Product eligibility or model mandates Protocol governance Version activation, validator/economic policy decisions Private customer financial operations Software release Artifact production and deployment candidate Live activation absent required gates AI/model governance Model approval, purpose, release state, monitoring Ledger writes or governance signatures Financial operations Treasury/settlement workflows under controls Bypass of canonical ledger or compliance decisions"},
  {"type": "callout", "label": "Threshold Authority", "text": "High-impact changes should require multiple independent evidence and approval domains, not merely multiple signatures controlled by the same operational pathway."},
  {"type": "section", "n": "28", "label": "", "title": "Post-Quantum Migration Roadmap"},
  {"type": "p", "text": "The transition should be managed as a security program with inventory, classification, qualification and controlled activation. CISA/NSA/NIST guidance emphasizes starting with cryptographic discovery and inventory because organizations often lack visibility into where public-key cryptography is embedded. CISA has continued this work with automated PQC discovery and inventory strategy. [E6][E7] SunRey's migration roadmap follows the same operational logic."},
  {"type": "figure", "caption": "Figure 8. Six-phase post-quantum migration roadmap.", "src": "/papers/figures/10-sunrey-post-quantum-security-fig-08.webp", "w": 1600, "h": 933},
  {"type": "p", "text": "28.1 Phase I - Cryptographic discovery Inventory TLS, certificates, PKI, API authentication, workload identity, blockchain signatures, wallet/custody signing, validator identity, governance signatures, database encryption, backups, code/release signing, HSM/KMS dependencies and third-party providers. 28.2 Phase II - Classification Prioritize by secrecy lifetime, authentication criticality, asset value, migration complexity, interoperability, hardware/provider support and performance constraints. 28.3 Phase III - Crypto-agility enforcement Remove unnecessary algorithm coupling; centralize algorithm policy and provider interfaces; version protocol suites; make deprecation and fallback observable. 28.4 Phase IV - Hybrid PQC qualification Evaluate standards-based PQC/hybrid mechanisms in isolated environments. Measure key/signature size, latency, CPU, memory, bandwidth, block-size impact, validator throughput, mobile performance, HSM/KMS/custody support and failure behavior. 28.5 Phase V - Controlled migration Promote selected paths through research → sandbox/testnet → preproduction → controlled production only when interoperability, operations, evidence and independent review are sufficient. 28.6 Phase VI - Legacy deprecation Retire quantum-vulnerable mechanisms only after dependent systems are migrated or explicitly risk-accepted and rollback/recovery paths are understood."},
  {"type": "section", "n": "29", "label": "", "title": "Post-Quantum Maturity Model"},
  {"type": "p", "text": "A maturity model is more defensible than a binary claim that the platform is or is not \"post-quantum.\" Level Definition Evidence threshold 0 - Unknown Cryptographic inventory incomplete Discovery gaps documented 1 - Crypto-agile architecture Algorithms/providers can be versioned and changed Registry, interfaces, policy and tests 2 - PQC-capable components validated Selected PQ primitives work in controlled components Vectors, interoperability/performance tests 3 - Hybrid PQC qualified Hybrid mechanisms operate in qualified environments End-to-end sandbox/preproduction evidence 4 - Selected production domains PQ-protected Specific live paths use qualified PQ/hybrid mechanisms Production configuration + monitoring + assurance 5 - Broad migration with residual-risk register Most priority quantum-vulnerable dependencies retired or bounded Enterprise inventory, independent verification, exceptions and deprecation plan Based on current source material, SunRey can credibly describe its design as **crypto-agile with post-quantum migration foundations** and report specific sandbox/testnet component work when reverified. The architecture does not support a blanket Level 4 or Level 5 claim across all production domains. [S4]"},
  {"type": "callout", "label": "Recommended Public Language", "text": "\"SunRey is designed as a crypto-agile financial and blockchain platform with a standards-based post-quantum migration architecture.\""},
  {"type": "section", "n": "30", "label": "", "title": "External Assurance and Production Gates"},
  {"type": "p", "text": "Internal engineering evidence is necessary; independent evidence is required for credible production claims. SunRey's own productization audit correctly identifies security audit, penetration test/retest, protocol review, HSM/KMS attestations and production key ceremonies as external launch gates. It also distinguishes current architecture from production provider, validator, legal and operational inputs. [S6] 30.1 External assurance package Independent application/API penetration test and remediation retest. Blockchain consensus/protocol and economic-state review. Cryptographic design and implementation review, including hybrid/PQC configuration where used. HSM/KMS/custody provider evidence and key-management assessment. Cloud/infrastructure and IAM assessment. Supply-chain/release provenance review. Privacy/HIN architecture review and data-flow assessment. Incident-response and recovery exercise with evidence. Validator operator security assessment and signing-process review. Production cryptographic inventory with unresolved dependency register."},
  {"type": "callout", "label": "Assurance Boundary", "text": "A green CI pipeline, a testnet or an internal red-team suite does not equal independent certification. Public claims"},
  {"type": "p", "text": "should name the assessed scope, version, environment and date."},
  {"type": "section", "n": "31", "label": "", "title": "Strategic Differentiation"},
  {"type": "p", "text": "The potentially distinctive security idea is the integration of cryptographic trust with economic authority boundaries. None of the individual ingredients - TLS, HSMs, KMS, zero trust, PQC, BFT consensus, policy engines, evidence stores or AI guardrails - should be claimed as individually novel. The potentially differentiating architecture lies in how these controls are composed around a sovereign economic system. 31.1 Integrated security fabric Crypto-agile protocol and service architecture with explicit PQ migration state. AI proposal/execution separation enforced by deterministic authorization. Proof of Permission spanning identity, authority, jurisdiction, compliance, risk and purpose. Native blockchain state that recognizes rights, consent, provenance, AI/machine identity and policy context. Protected off-chain human information with rights/evidence anchored to the economic system. Provider evidence that is useful but not self-authorizing. Canonical ledger/chain/custody ownership boundaries preventing shadow financial truth. Evidence-producing execution and recovery, linking security operations to reconstructable economic events. Governance separation so keys, releases and models cannot silently become monetary or protocol authority."},
  {"type": "callout", "label": "Potential Innovation Thesis", "text": "A sovereign economic infrastructure in which cryptography, identity, policy, AI authority, economic execution and"},
  {"type": "p", "text": "evidence operate as one security fabric. Formal novelty or patentability would require separate prior-art analysis."},
  {"type": "section", "n": "32", "label": "", "title": "Conclusion"},
  {"type": "p", "text": "Post-quantum security is not an algorithm swap; it is the preservation of trustworthy economic authority as technology evolves. The central security problem of an AI-native economic system is not only whether an attacker can read encrypted information. It is whether any compromised component can acquire authority it was never intended to possess. SunRey's architecture addresses this by separating reasoning from permission, permission from execution, execution from canonical state, and canonical state from the evidence used to reconstruct it. Post-quantum cryptography strengthens that architecture by preparing the public-key trust layer for a future change in cryptographic assumptions. NIST's standardized ML-KEM, ML-DSA and SLH-DSA, modern KEM guidance, 2026 crypto-agility guidance and IETF hybrid TLS standardization now provide a substantially stronger external foundation for migration planning than existed only a few years ago. [E1-E5][E8]"},
  {"type": "callout", "label": "Final Thesis", "text": "Post-quantum security is the ability of an economic system to preserve confidentiality, authenticity, authorization"},
  {"type": "p", "text": "and monetary integrity as cryptographic assumptions, infrastructure and autonomous agents evolve. SunRey's strongest security claim is therefore not that it has eliminated cyber risk. It is that the architecture is being designed so security decisions remain explicit, bounded, replaceable, evidence-producing and independently verifiable from user identity and AI proposals through blockchain consensus, financial execution, key management and operational recovery. A | GLOSSARY Glossary Term Definition ActionIntent Structured request or proposal describing a consequential action before authorization. Compliance Kernel Deterministic control plane evaluating identity, authority, jurisdiction, compliance, risk and purpose. Execution Authority Signed, bounded and short-lived authorization accepted by controlled execution infrastructure. Crypto agility Capability to replace/adapt cryptographic algorithms while preserving security and operations. KEM Key-encapsulation mechanism used to establish a shared secret over a public channel."},
  {"type": "callout", "label": "Ml-Kem", "text": "NIST-standardized module-lattice KEM in FIPS 203."},
  {"type": "callout", "label": "Ml-Dsa", "text": "NIST-standardized module-lattice digital signature algorithm in FIPS 204."},
  {"type": "callout", "label": "Slh-Dsa", "text": "NIST-standardized stateless hash-based digital signature algorithm in FIPS 205."},
  {"type": "p", "text": "Hybrid PQ/traditional Use of both a post-quantum and a conventional mechanism so security can remain robust during migration. KeyProvider SunRey abstraction separating application logic from specific key-management/signing implementations. Sovereign Cell Regional deployment boundary with local data, keys, policies, ledgers/legal entities and recovery infrastructure. Evidence Vault Tamper-evident/WORM-oriented evidence store supporting reconstruction and audit. HIN Human Information Network - rights-governed architecture for authorized human information and contribution. Canonical state Authoritative ledger/blockchain/custody state that other systems may reference but not duplicate as competing truth. B | REFERENCES References Internal SunRey architecture sources [S1] Solstice Architecture & Feature Reference, Version 1.0, August 13, 2026. Historical Solstice naming; public terminology superseded by SunRey. [S2] Master Build Instructions for the Solstice Global AI Financial Operating System, August 2026. Historical naming; used for canonical security, Proof of Permission, Vault, custody, resilience and policy requirements. [S3] Solstice SFF 2.0 Architecture Summary, August 2026. Used for the deterministic authorization and Regulatory Digital Twin model. [S4] SunRey Comprehensive Build Report, August 18, 2026. Used for crypto-agility, post-quantum migration foundations, chain/validator security, HSM/KMS ports, assurance and production-gate distinctions. [S5] SunRey Blockchain & Universal Economic Exchange - Technical Architecture and Novel Market Design, August 2026. Used for sovereign-chain security domains, rich economic state, identity/rights/provenance/policy and interoperability. [S6] SunRey Backend Productization Audit and Next Steps, August 23, 2026. Used for provider runtime, zero-trust hardening, software supply chain, SRE/DR and external assurance gates. External standards and guidance [E1] NIST FIPS 203, Module-Lattice-Based Key-Encapsulation Mechanism Standard, August 13, 2024. https://doi.org/10.6028/NIST.FIPS.203 [E2] NIST FIPS 204, Module-Lattice-Based Digital Signature Standard, August 13, 2024. https://doi.org/10.6028/NIST.FIPS.204 [E3] NIST FIPS 205, Stateless Hash-Based Digital Signature Standard, August 13, 2024. https://doi.org/10.6028/NIST.FIPS.205 [E4] NIST SP 800-227, Recommendations for Key-Encapsulation Mechanisms, September 2025. https://doi.org/10.6028/NIST.SP.800-227 [E5] NIST CSWP 39upd1, Considerations for Achieving Crypto Agility: Strategies and Practices, June 29, 2026. https://doi.org/10.6028/NIST.CSWP.39-upd1 [E6] CISA / NSA / NIST, Quantum-Readiness: Migration to Post-Quantum Cryptography, August 17, 2023. [E7] CISA, Strategy for Migrating to Automated Post-Quantum Cryptography Discovery and Inventory Tools, September 2024. [E8] IETF RFC 10024, Post-Quantum Traditional (PQ/T) Hybrid Key Agreement Mechanisms for TLS 1.3, August 2026. [E9] NIST SP 800-207, Zero Trust Architecture, August 2020. https://doi.org/10.6028/NIST.SP.800-207 [E10] NIST SP 800-218, Secure Software Development Framework (SSDF) Version 1.1, February 2022. https://doi.org/10.6028/NIST.SP.800-218 [E11] NSA, Commercial National Security Algorithm Suite 2.0 (CNSA 2.0) FAQ, Version 2.1, December 2024; and NSA Post-Quantum Cybersecurity Resources."},
  {"type": "callout", "label": "Reference Note", "text": "External references are used for standards context and fact-checking. They do not imply NIST, CISA, NSA or IETF"},
  {"type": "p", "text": "endorsement of SunRey."}
];
